```

<<~ aka "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/RFC-2119#/normative-language">>

<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

! The Founding Runbook ~ the go-live rite

> A woken operator runs this rite to stand a node fresh-from-void, found the three-kahu
> quorum, arm the charter's pre-rotation, and go live. Each step reads as its own verb; the
> irreversible one wears a warning. The rite runs top to bottom once — a re-run re-founds.

<<~ ahu #/the-rite>>

!! The rite ~ rehearsed first, then the six movements, then the growing

<<~ loops "⓪ rehearse -> ✶ clear -> ⏿ found -> ◇ reserve -> ▶ seal-and-back-up -> ↺ seed -> LIVE -> ⚲ crossroads -> ⊕ grow -> ⊛ gather -> ⊙ feed -> ⊘ dwell">>

''Rehearse'' runs the whole rite in containers, disposably, until it bores (#/rehearse) · ''clear''
pares the vessel to void · ''found'' stands the device + the three kahu · ''reserve'' forges the
pre-rotation's future-key custody · ''seal-and-back-up'' locks the secrets at rest + writes a
recoverable copy · ''seed'' plants the content from `bags/` · ''LIVE'' serves ·
''crossroads'' stands a Herm where a family needs one (#/crossroads) · ''grow'' opens the Nexus,
contracts fellow operators, and welcomes users at the anon floor (#/grow-the-nexus) · ''gather'' brings the
operator's own vessels in as contracted leaves (#/gather-the-fleet) · ''feed'' lays the first offering, which
IS the realm's founding (#/feed-the-realm) · ''dwell'' seats a second operator IN the realm rather than
merely carrying for it (#/dwell).

''The last three movements run AFTER LIVE and never gate it'' — and they carry different readiness. ⊛ Gather
runs today on built verbs, ⊙ Feed among them (its two doors landed 2026-08-07). ⊘ Dwell holds a
built vouch and a crossing that REFUSES until the operator seats the admission dials — and its conversion act
stays un-modelled by ruling, so no command there may name a quorum, a waiting period, or a test.

''THE RITE RUNS IN TWO BRANCHES, and they want opposite virtues'' (operator ruling, 2026-07-27).
A ''REHEARSAL'' founding runs in containers, disposably, as many times as it takes — cheapness IS
the point and nothing stands worth backing up. A ''KEEPER'' founding runs once, on the real hearth,
irreversibly. ''Movements ②–⑨ read the same in both''; the branches part at ① and at what a failure
COSTS. Every rehearsal therefore doubles as a dress rehearsal of the keeper — same commands, same
order, disposable stakes — which stops the keeper from ever being a first take.

''THE RITE STANDS ONE VESSEL; THE ARC RUNS WIDER.'' What happens across several vessels, several operators and however long the months take — a realm FED after LIVE, a fleet gathered as contracted leaves, a second operator founding independently and then DWELLING IN the first realm — rides at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/first-realm-arc">>. It names three stretches this rite does not walk: the fleet join (② names the CONTRACTED mode and walks only SELF-STOOD), the realm feeding (no movement names the first offering, and `feedCabalRealm` stands built and unwalked), and the dwelling join (⊕ Grow contracts CARRIAGE and seats no DWELLER — carriage and dwelling ride orthogonal axes).

''Read this rite as the operator-instruction source.'' Each movement names its branch, its exact
commands, and its precondition, so a reader (human or agent) can answer //what do I run next// from
the vessel's current state rather than from memory (#/agent-seed).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/rehearse>>

!! ⓪ Rehearse ~ run it in a box until it bores (the REHEARSAL branch opens here)

''A ceremony nobody has performed carries no evidence, only intent.'' This rite stood `proposed` and
unrun while the code beneath it moved for a week — so the first movement stands one that costs
nothing to repeat. The harness already stands: `docker-compose.mesh.yml` founds ''real'' identities
in containers (`tools/lararium-container-boot.sh` — //founds its OWN identity, then composeHerm//)
with two Herm relays on a shared network.

```
docker compose -f docker-compose.mesh.yml up --abort-on-container-exit   # found, in a box
tools/herm-mesh-witness.sh                                               # witness the crossroads carry
docker compose -f docker-compose.mesh.yml down -v                        # THE CLEAR — no backup, nothing kept
```

''The burn IS the clear, and that inverts the danger.'' Where the keeper branch opens on the most
destructive command in the house, the rehearsal branch opens on one whose whole virtue reads
//repeatable//. Run it until the sequence bores; ''boredom names the readiness the keeper needs''.

!!! The KEEPER rehearsal ~ the sequence this branch never ran

''The container harness stands the MESH branch'' — it founds vessels with `lares vessel found` and witnesses Herm
carry + partition. ''Movements ②–⑥ were never run end-to-end by anything'': persona → nexus seal → vault →
regenesis → live had no green baseline to diff a failure against. `tools/rehearse-keeper.sh` gives them one.

```
tools/rehearse-keeper.sh                             # 2 cycles, throwaway tree, burns on exit
tools/rehearse-keeper.sh --cycles 5 --keep           # run until it bores; keep the tree to inspect
```

''THE REHEARSAL NOW WALKS ⑦ — the door a second operator comes through.'' Movements ②–⑥ prove a hearth
standing ALONE, and a Nexus that cannot admit a second operator is a private vessel wearing a charter. ⑦
runs both halves of the handshake with held personas (`accept-carriage` mints the joiner's contract-in,
the quorum counter-signs it onto the members board, `revoke` supersedes) ''and the refusal'': a nym with
no contract-in must fail closed and write nothing. A gate only ever shown saying yes has not been shown
to be a gate.

''AND THE CUSTODY CROSSING HAS ITS OWN WITNESS.'' ⑦ signs with a persona the same vessel holds, so the
one property the ceremony exists for — that a Nexus admits a key it has ''NEVER HELD'' — goes unmeasured
there. `contract-witness` stands TWO roots that share nothing and crosses it for real:

```
tools/contract-witness.sh                            # two independent foundings, one handshake
npm run witness                                      # every witness, never short-circuiting
```

''It also surfaces a step one vessel hides.'' `accept-carriage` reads the roster from the JOINER'S OWN
seal home, because a contract-in binds to the charter epoch it consents under — so ''B cannot consent to
a charter it has never seen'', and A's `founding-roster.mem` (public material: seated keys, threshold,
epoch lineage) must travel FIRST. Holding it lets B name the epoch; it grants B no quorum, because the
public charter is not a key.

''AND THE JOINER KEEPS WHAT SHE SIGNED.'' A relation has two sides and ''each side holds its own
evidence'': the founder's is the admit on her members board, the joiner's is her own contract-in.
`accept-carriage` writes that consent beside the charter it binds to, so a vessel reads the relation
it stands in ''without holding any partner's document''. The record is EPOCH-BOUND — a consent rooted
behind the standing epoch consented to terms that have since moved, and grants nothing.

''AND THE RECORD PROVES ITSELF.'' Disk holds no trust: `LAR_ROOT` names the whole seal home, so a
reading that trusted the file's PRESENCE would report a Nexus a vessel never joined. Three things
carry it — ''the epoch stands'', ''the seal verifies'' (it binds nym and epoch together, and only the
holder of that nym's seed can make it), and ''the nym is a root THIS vessel holds''. The third is the
one that hides: another operator's consent is genuine evidence that //she// joined, and copying it
would claim a relation somebody else entered. Trust rides the signature, never the location.

''The members board records, and each vessel decides.'' A stamp is a ''shared contract into the Cabal'' —
a public, quorum-countersigned fact about who stamped in — so the board belongs on the per-Nexus
shared side beside the KEL, the antigen, who-face and crossroads. ''Whether THIS vessel carries for a
given member stays this vessel's own reading OVER that record.'' Sharing a record conscripts nobody;
fusing the record with the decision would, because an admit only ever WIDENS whom a vessel carries
for and an operator consents at ONE epoch, never to every admit made after it.

⚠ ''What an artifact that TRAVELS may carry.'' A charter travels before a partner can consent, and a
shared board carries the stamps that contracted in — so whichever key class they name is ''published,
and a key published once cannot be unpublished''. Three key classes stand and they do not
interchange: a ''vessel key'' is device-minted and must never co-surface; a ''PersonaGroup root'' binds
one human's own devices into an overlay; a ''veiled-user Handle'' is the sovereign pseudonym presented
through the veil, and it is the only one of the three meant to be seen.

''The Handle carries the same verification and none of the exposure.'' A PersonaGroup root on a
travelling artifact names that human's device-group — and several drawn from one vault correlate to
each other, which is what a founding seat does when it draws every chair from one identity home. An
unrecognised key reads UNKNOWN rather than safe: a vessel cannot classify a key it did not mint, and a
travelling artifact is the wrong place to guess.

⚠ ''AND PUBLISHABLE IS NOT THE WHOLE QUESTION.'' A Handle is MEANT to be seen — a HandleCard announces
it, and recognition needs it ''stable''. That same stability ''correlates a human across every circle
it stands in''. The persona tree already carries the floor one level down: `m / handle' / context' /
circle-scope'`, the scope index derived from the circle's own doc id, so ''the same persona presents a
different key to each circle it joins'' and rejoining the same circle returns the same key.

''An artifact scoped to ONE circle wants the SCOPED leaf.'' A stamp re-keyed to a bare Handle would
drop the device-overlay and keep the cross-circle link — a fix shaped like one.

''An ISLAND takes its own domain, never the circle's.'' Canon sets the shape — //the name DERIVES from
the compartment's own material; each plane answers to a domain-separated MAC over that PersonaGroup's
own doc id// — and a circle names a human's own compartment where a Nexus names an island several
operators share. One MAC domain across both would let a compartment leaf collide with an island leaf,
which the levels stay apart to forbid. An island answers to its ''genesis epoch AID'', content-addressed
over the seated key-set, so the leaf derives from the island's own material and from nothing a vessel
owns.


''ONE ENV VAR ISOLATES EVERYTHING.'' `LAR_ROOT` re-roots data, state, the seal, the acquired library, bags
and genesis — eleven by re-siting, and the UDS socket ''by DERIVATION'': the rendezvous stands at
`/tmp/lares-<uid>/<digest-of-root>.sock`, OUTSIDE every root and every login session, because a lararium
serves as civic infrastructure and its daemon outlives the operator logging out (operator-ruled
2026-08-23) — while the digest keeps two roots off one socket. ''TWELVE of twelve'', re-measured after
the rendezvous re-siting: a clean `vessel stop` unlinks its socket; a hard kill leaves a dead name the
next contact reaps. So a throwaway costs no containers and
destroys nothing real; the container harness stays for what containers actually buy (rude sockets, real
disks, a drop that drops). ''The count gets RE-MEASURED whenever a resolver moves, never carried forward'' —
an isolation claim that ages is an isolation claim that lies, and the tenth resource (the TCP port) was
found exactly that way. The harness ''REFUSES'' any root under the operator's home, any root naming the
repo, and any root already carrying an identity — ''a rehearsal harness that can eat the hearth is not one''.

''THE SEED SET'' (operator-approved 2026-08-08): `lares` · `lararium` · `crossroads` — the two system
bags plus the public floor, re-copied EACH cycle so cycle 2 tests the ''rite's'' idempotency rather than a
warm tree's. `LAR_ROOT` isolates AND empties `bags/`, so a run that skipped this would prove less than it
appears to.

''IT RUNS THE RITE AS WRITTEN, because founding reaches nothing outside the vessel.'' A standup that also
pip-installed the mempalace library and stood the sensorium organs would write OUTSIDE `LAR_ROOT` and make
an isolated founding impossible. ''The mempalace is a SIDECAR'' (operator ruling, 2026-08-08) and its own
header always said so. Both legs carry their own doors:

```
lares mempalace install                              # the library deps (submodule + pip)
lares sense setup                                    # the sovereign sensorium organs
```

''A vessel founds and serves carrying neither.'' A stand still REPORTS what stands and counsels the verbs,
because naming a missing tool serves the operator and installing one behind them does not.

!!! ⚠ THE UNATTENDED-REBOOT TENSION ~ named at the gate, unruled

''A sealed vessel that reboots with nobody awake REFUSES TO BOOT today.'' For one operator's laptop that
reads fine; for a crossroads a family depends on, ''the at-rest seal converts a power cut into an outage''
— and the seal exists to protect a stolen disk, never to gate an ordinary restart.

The option space and its costs live beside the reading itself (`archive-passphrase#archiveOpens`), so a
later hand meets it where the decision happens rather than in a document beside it. ''BUILT NOW'': a hearth
whose archive holds shut STANDS at the waking floor as a Herm — faceless by class, carrying, serving the
public shelf, every sovereign act closed — rather than refusing to boot (`standAs`, and the boot says so in
plain words). ''What remains UNBUILT is the unattended raise'', and one correction rides with it: the raise
is a ①-VESSEL act held by whoever holds that vessel's opening secret, ''never a kahu quorum'' — a cabal is
seated from Handles standing on an already-raised lararium, so that dependency closes in a circle
(waking-floor#the-raise-is-a-vessel-layer-act). The shape that reads most pono: a vessel that cannot open its archive has lost its ''caps'', never its ''floor'' — so it
should come up ''anon at the floor'' (serving the public shelf, which needs no secret), hold every sovereign
act closed, say loudly what it cannot do, and let an ACT unseal it after. ''And that act already has a shape
the mesh runs'': a kahu quorum signing against the pre-rotated epoch chain, rather than a second authority
invented beside it.

''RULED'': passphrase-primary; no random key beside the ciphertext; never a silent degrade to weak keys.

''RULED and ENACTED'': an unattended boot ''stands at the floor''. `standAs(asked, archiveOpens)` returns the
asked class only where the identity archive opens and `"herm"` otherwise, so a vessel whose seal stays shut
meshes, carries, routes, and says loudly what it cannot do — it seats no persona
(`personaSlotCeiling("herm") === 0`). Nothing degrades silently, because the floor is a NAMED standing rather
than a weakened one: the vessel that cannot open its archive holds no key to misuse. An operator ''lights the
hearth fire'' after, and a recognised operator may RAISE caps onto it that ride their own keys and always fall
back (waking-floor). An outage recovers; a silently unsealed identity does not — and neither happens here.

A crossroads may now stand where nobody sleeps: it wakes at the floor and waits for a hand.

''TWO LEGS STILL GO UN-EXERCISED, named so a green run never reads as more than it proves'':
* `lares vessel stand --init` fans out to the AI-surface wires (`~/.claude` · `~/.codex` · `~/.copilot`). The harness
  passes `--install`, never `--init`, so ''the harness wiring goes untested here'' — a machine-setup concern
  rather than a founding one.
* the vault seals from `LARES_ARCHIVE_PASSPHRASE`, so ''the no-echo TTY prompt leg goes un-exercised''.

''What a rehearsal proves that a test cannot.'' The suites prove every property in-process, authored
by the same hand that wrote the code — the field has never disagreed. A container founding lets
sockets be rude, disks be real, and a drop actually drop: it stands the cheapest ''outside island''
available (#/the-frame). Deliberately in scope here: remote CAS transit to a holder outside this
process, bilateral genesis across two sovereign hearths, the Herm's re-key/re-share/heal teeth, and
reconnection after a real drop.

''FIRST RUN — 2026-07-27, and the rite amends itself as promised.'' Three cycles ran. ''2 green, 1 red.''
What the field said, recorded here because the run outranks the document:

# ''THE RITE'S OWN INSTRUMENTS OVERCLAIMED — twice, and neither failure reaches a test suite.'' The
  cycle runner declared `cycles green: 1/1` while BOTH witnesses failed (a bash `if` without `else`
  yields 0, so the status read the branch and never the command). The carry witness printed
  //verified at each hop// while a hop read UNVERIFIED (its gate checks the LAST hop alone). Both cured;
  the runner now ''proves its own detector'' before trusting a cycle, and refuses to proceed when a
  forced failure fails to register. ''A verdict MUST state its own scope, or a green measures the
  instrument's silence.''
# ''AN INTERMITTENT FAIL-CLOSED BINDING GATE, ~1 cold founding in 3.'' `herm-relay-2` exits 1 with
  //persona-KEL chain for the pinned identifier … absent from the local board replica — the Binding
  Gate cannot reach a head (fail-closed)//. A second identical run stands all four vessels clean. ''A
  RACE, never a structural break'' — precisely the class one test run cannot see and a repeated
  rehearsal does. Surfaced, deliberately undiagnosed: the Binding Gate sits in live work, and
  #found already names this failure shape as the cost of founding without a carried chain.
# ''WHAT PASSED, and it matters.'' On a healthy mesh the announced dial crosses source → relay-1 →
  relay-2 with the pointer signed, the snapshot hash-matched and the dial DECODED; and the partition
  witness confirms relay-2 keeps advancing after the witness CUTS relay-1 — federation riding carried dials,
  never a hardcoded peer list.

''The rite therefore does NOT yet stand WALKED.'' One movement stands amended (this one), and a red cycle
stands unexplained. ''Boredom has not arrived.''

''The rehearsal amends this meme; this meme does not predict the rehearsal.'' Where a run contradicts
a movement below, ''the run wins'' and the movement gets rewritten — the rite stands WALKED only after a
rehearsal completes end-to-end ''without amending it''.

''The bar reads as a silent document, never a green report.'' A run that
passes while teaching the rite something has not walked it — it has repaired it. So the test asks whether
this meme needed to change, which no amount of green output can fake.

!!! SECOND PASS — 2026-08-04, and the rite amends again without a rehearsal

''A reading against live code found what a run would have found, so the same law applies.'' Three
amendments landed and one hypothesis got recorded:

# ''⓪′ PREFLIGHT ADDED, ahead of the irreversible clear.'' The toolchain went unchecked before a movement
  that cannot be undone. Measured: the entry point died at import on a stale build.
# ''⊛ GATHER'S COMMANDS CONTRADICTED ITS OWN PROSE.'' The text described a joining vessel minting its own
  key and receiving a signed edge — correct. The commands signed a payload before any joinee key existed
  — impossible: the signing call refuses without the joinee's public verifying key. ''The description
  tracked the design and the sequence tracked the past'', which reads as the most dangerous shape a
  runbook carries, because the prose reassures the reader that the commands were checked.
# ''THE ADMIT PAYLOAD'S CONTENTS NAMED.'' It carries one signed edge on a lineage, never a
  capability-event bundle (#/gather).
# ''A CANDIDATE FOR THE STANDING RED, unproven.'' The unexplained fail-closed Binding Gate reports a
  persona-KEL chain absent from the local board replica. An admit payload measured 2026-08-04 carries a
  chain of ''length 1''. A one-event chain against an unsynced replica reads as a plausible
  cannot-reach-a-head shape. ''NOT reproduced'' — recorded so the next
  rehearsal watches the chain length, never as a diagnosis.

''The corroboration, with its limit named.'' The founding-then-admit path stands green end-to-end in an
automated witness (9 of 9, 2026-08-04). ''That witness is not this rite'' — it stands no containers, its
sockets stay polite, and no drop actually drops (#/rehearse). It corroborates the SHAPE of movements ②
and ⊛; it substitutes for no cycle.

!!! THIRD PASS — 2026-08-06, the seat stops reading a private label

''A reading of the rite against the identity plane found the movement-② join reaching for the wrong
name.'' The seal matched each chair against the operator's ''PRIVATE pet-name'', which welded two
registers the plane holds apart: an operator could seat only under the string they call the compartment
at home, so every private label became a public commitment by construction, and a compartment called
a compartment labelled `the-burner` could never stand under any other name.

''Nothing had leaked'' — the published string always came from the seal's own seed — so the finding names
a ''forced coupling'', never a disclosure. The correction gives the outward intent its own store and its
own act (`--handle`), and the chair claim a third (`--seat`), and re-points the join at the declared
Handle. Movement ② carries the new sequence; ⊛ gather gains `persona sync`, because both names now ride
the human's own fleet.

''What a rehearsal watches here.'' That the two registers stay ''visibly different'' — set a private
label that reads nothing like the chair name and confirm the seat still lands. A rehearsal that types the
same string into both flags proves nothing, because a label-matching join would pass it too.

''AND THE SAME READING FOUND TWO MORE, both un-pono for the same reason'' (operator, 2026-08-07):

# ''THE FOUNDING NAMES SHIPPED IN THE BUILD.'' A `FOUNDING_KAHU` constant carried three display names, so
  the SOURCE decided who the founding kahu were and the operator merely confirmed it. ''Retired.'' The
  scaffold now names nobody, and the roster forms at the seat from the personas that declared and stood.
  The threshold followed the same cut: a `2` written into a file would seat one Nexus's fairness call inside
  every Nexus that founds, so the seat derives majority and takes `--threshold` for any realm wanting another.
# ''THE PRIOR SEAL SURVIVES A CLEAR.'' `lares vessel clear --force` preserves the identity home and the `rm -rf` cuts
  it; ''neither touches `bags/`'', where the charter doc lives. A re-founding that skipped it stranded itself
  on a fail-closed lineage. ① now names the cut (#/clear).

# ''THE SEAL SAT IN THE REPOSITORY.'' Sited in the corpus bags tree, it inherited that tree's home — which on
  a development install sits inside the working tree, so a founding seal could land in a git history. ''The
  seal now homes per-operator'' at `<lares>/nexus`, beside the sovereign identity and behind the same
  reset boundary. ① cuts both (#/clear).

''The shared shape.'' Each named ''a thing decided somewhere the operator does not stand'' — a private label
deciding a public chair, a build deciding a roster, a stale artifact deciding a lineage, a directory default
deciding where a Nexus lives. ''A founding an operator merely confirms reads as somebody else's founding.''

!!! FIFTH PASS — 2026-08-09, the ground clears and a coined name fails its check

''The deps came current and the field said one thing worth keeping.'' Every runtime pin the operator approved
already stood at latest; the only live bump ran `automerge-repo` alpha.2 → alpha.3 (a `uuid` and an `xstate`
patch range, nothing else), plus the dev tooling. ''Build green, 1682 specs + 3318 tests, zero failures.''

''ONE TEST FELL, AND IT NAMED AN OLDER MISTAKE THAN THE BUMP.'' A case asserting the `lar:` root's arity
measured it by ''counting slash-segments''. Re-homing a meme one directory shallower dropped the count, so a
passing assertion turned false without the law it guarded moving at all. It now reads the arity the law
actually governs — three dot-terms — and no future re-homing can make it lie. ''A test that measures a proxy
for its law reports on the proxy'', and the day the two part company it accuses the innocent change.

''THE POINTERS OUTSIDE THE REPO CARRIED A DEAD GENERATION.'' A sweep of the machine's AI-instruction surfaces
found every `lar:` URI in `~/.claude/skills` naming `@lares/v0.1/...` — a path family that stopped existing at
the bags move and had ''dangled silently ever since''. Eighteen URIs, all repointed and each verified against
a real `.mem` before writing. Six SKILL memes in the corpus carried the same rot in `skill-package-root`.
''Transcripts and editor history stay untouched by law'' — rewriting a record to match the present falsifies it.

''A dangling pointer in an instruction file fails silently and reads
as fine'', because nothing resolves it until a reader tries — and a reader who cannot resolve it invents.

!!!! ⚠ `hoʻāla` FAILS ITS LEXICON CHECK — the concept stands, the pet-name does not

''The Hawaiian Lexicon Committee already coined `hoʻāla` for computing, and it means the opposite of ours.''
//Māmaka Kaiao// assigns it ''to format, initialize, as a computer disk'' (`hoʻāla hou` = //to reformat//).
Our sense named the ''waking floor'' — a vessel coming up at its minimum cap-stack, ''preserving everything''.

''In this rite the collision turns dangerous rather than merely awkward'': a founder meets `hoʻāla` a few
lines from the irreversible clear (#/clear), and a ʻŌlelo Hawaiʻi reader would take the two for one family.
''A name that inverts its own meaning beside a destructive command is a trap, not a label.''

''RULED'': the pet-name goes, and the concept keeps its grounding (Direct Boot BFU/AFU · SPIRE · kubelet
TLS bootstrap · Tang/Clevis) and its meme. ''It now reads THE WAKING FLOOR'' — plain English, operator-ruled,
and the phrase the prose carries throughout, so handle and body now say one thing.

''THE CHECK RAN WIDER THAN THE ONE WORD, and every near candidate failed too.'' Read against the primary
dictionaries rather than a summary: `hoʻomaka` carries ''to begin, start, as a computer program'' — spent,
and pointed at //launch// where this names a //floor//, so it fails twice over; `kahua` carries ''field, as
in a database'', a collision a Hawaiian-speaking developer would meet daily; `paepae` stands FREE in the
compute domain and reads almost perfectly (//support · prop · threshold · the plate the rafters rest on//),
but ''Paepae o Heʻeia'' already carries that exact metaphor for a Native Hawaiian nonprofit stewarding an
800-year-old fishpond, so taking it would repeat the pattern rather than mend it.

''The lesson generalizes past this name.'' Kōmike Huaʻōlelo entered
the computing domain DELIBERATELY and keeps coining in it, so a term minted from outside either collides
with a decision they made or pre-empts one they have not made yet. ''Plain English costs nothing here'' —
the house carries registers of its own (Discordian, Elyncian) where the fiction stays ours to move.
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor">>

!!! ★ WALKED — 2026-08-09, and the rite changed nothing ★

''Every movement stood, across two cycles, and this meme needed no amendment.'' By the bar set above, the
rite stands ''WALKED'': not a green report, but a ''silent document''. Cycle 2 re-ran from the top on a
cleared tree, so the idempotency claim reads tested rather than asserted. 2m36s against the prior run's
4m28s, because nothing rebuilt mid-cycle.

''FOUR OF THE FIVE FAILURES THAT GOT HERE WERE INSTRUMENTS, NOT THE RITE'' — and each one had reported
green, or reported red against something innocent:

<<~ has Found Impatience "read/no socket after 60s truth/the CLI budgets 120 for a cold boot ~ the harness out-waited what it measured">>
